--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Design influences and attribution
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Bindery was designed after surveying the following projects. Ideas and
architectural approaches are not subject to copyright; the acknowledgements
below record intellectual debt, not code reuse.

* Memorix (https://github.com/AVIDS2/memorix) - Apache-2.0
  Influence: the observation that MCP tool schemas themselves consume context
  tokens, and the resulting discipline of exposing a deliberately small tool
  surface. Bindery exposes six tools for this reason.

* codebase-memory-mcp (https://github.com/DeusData/codebase-memory-mcp) - MIT
  Influence: the framing that structural questions should be answered from a
  prebuilt index rather than by having the agent read files one by one.

* mem0 (https://github.com/mem0ai/mem0) - Apache-2.0
  Influence: the separation of a durable memory store from the agent runtime.

* Basic Memory (https://github.com/basicmachines-co/basic-memory) - AGPL-3.0
  Influence ONLY. No code from Basic Memory is present in this project, and
  none was copied, adapted, or translated. The shared idea - that plain
  Markdown files on disk should remain the source of truth so the notes stay
  greppable, diffable, and usable by humans in Obsidian - is a design concept.
  Bindery is licensed Apache-2.0 and is NOT a derivative work of
  Basic Memory.

* ByteRover CLI / Cipher (https://github.com/campfirein/byterover-cli) - Elastic License 2.0
  Reviewed for design comparison only. ELv2 is not an open source license, so
  no code from it was used, and Bindery intentionally omits the cloud
  sync and team-workspace features that project centers on.

No source code from any of the above projects is included in this
distribution.
